Report what PARDISO needs, and let it factor in single precision.

TWO PROBLEMS, ONE CAUSE.  ccx fuses PARDISO's analysis and numerical
factorisation into phase 12, so the only way to learn what a deck costs was
to run the whole factorisation and watch it fail.  At L_mesh 0.0080 that
meant seven hours of out-of-core thrashing -- 21.9 TB read against a 131 GB
factor, the same factor re-read 166 times -- before the machine admitted it
could not do it.  PARDISO knows after the ANALYSIS phase alone, in seconds.

  pardiso.c   CCX_PARDISO_MEMREPORT=1     report, then factor as usual
              CCX_PARDISO_MEMREPORT=stop  report and stop, to size a machine

Phase 12 is split into 11 then 22, and iparm(15)/(16)/(17) are read between
them: the symbolic peak, the part of it that stays allocated, and the
numerical factorisation plus solve.  Peak is max(iparm(15), iparm(16)+
iparm(17)), reported for the mtype actually in use -- which matters, because
the out-of-core branch upgrades mtype 1 to 11.

  L_mesh   equations   mtype           PEAK
  0.0240      177 848  1  (in core)    5.12 GB
  0.0240      177 848  11 (OOC)        5.37 GB
  0.0080    1 691 831  1              136.37 GB

That last number is the whole story of the 0.0080 failure: a 24 GB core
budget was asked to hold a 136 GB factor.  Memory grows about as neq^1.46
across those two points, which puts L_mesh 0.0060 near 400 GB.

SINGLE PRECISION.  CCX_PARDISO_SINGLE=1 sets iparm(28)=1 and halves the term
that decides whether a deck fits.

Setting iparm(28) alone does nothing, silently.  The default path leaves
iparm(1)=0, "ignore iparm and use the defaults", so iparm(28) is discarded;
PARDISO then factors in DOUBLE while being handed the float array, and
segfaults.  Reaching iparm(28) needs iparm(1)=1, and pardisoinit is the only
supported way to get a consistent set to modify -- the same requirement the
out-of-core patch documents.  MKL also reads the VALUE arrays at the declared
precision, so the matrix and the right-hand side are converted around the
call; the matrix copy is a few GB against a factor of over a hundred.

IT IS AN APPROXIMATION.  MKL PARDISO in single mode refines in single too, so
accuracy caps near 1e-7 relative, and an F-bar ES-FEM system at nu ~ 0.4999
is ill-conditioned enough that this is not obviously safe.  So it is measured,
not assumed, against the acceptance deck's exact double-precision reaction:

  L_mesh 0.0240, undrained, c=1     wall     peak RSS   F_x on RP-1
  double                           68.46 s   6.26 GB    1.552992E+07
  single                           43.35 s   3.83 GB    1.552994E+07

39% less memory, 37% less time, and the reaction agrees to 1.3 ppm --
E_x = 6.212 GPa either way.  On the 0.0080 deck the reported factor falls from
136.37 GB to 72.33 GB, a clean 1.9x.

The conversion is done IN PLACE.  A float is half a double, so writing
fp[i] at offset 4i can never overwrite a double at offset 8i that has not
been read yet; the array is then shrunk.  The obvious two-array version
holds both at once, which on the 0.0060 cell is 6.07 GB of double beside
3.04 GB of float -- a 3 GB peak paid for nothing.

Both are OPT-IN and do nothing unless their variable is set.  Note that the
memory REPORT is precision-blind if taken before iparm(1)=1 is in force:
PARDISO sizes the factor symbolically, so a report that still says 136 GB
under CCX_PARDISO_SINGLE means iparm(28) never took effect.
